  "query_preview": "period of dry weather...MOS guidance is likely a few \ndegrees too cool during the afternoons and we have increased. \nAfternoon high temperatures will mainly be in the upper 60s to the \nlower 70s...although a few degrees cooler for the Cape/Islands. In \nfact...925T rising into the +11C to +15C range will potentially \nallow for some locations to reach the middle 70s especially towards \nmid-week and challenge record high temps. The main uncertainty on \ntemperatures will revolve around the amount of mid-high level clouds \nthat spill into the region at times. Regardless...high confidence in \ncontinued dry weather with unseasonably mild temperatures through \nthe middle of the week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pattern becomes more unsettled late-week into the \nweekend featuring periodic chances for rain. \n\nEnsemble guidance shows a consensus in a shift of the pattern late \nThursday into Friday as a mid-level trough arrives from the west. \nThis will advect in a plume of rich moisture 2-3 standard deviations \nabove normal. This will be accompanied by a frontal system that \npushes across the Ohio River Valley and into the Mid-Atlantic and \nNortheast. This will likely bring us our next best shot at \nprecipitation. Details such as timing, rain amounts/rates are still \nlower confidence due to uncertainty in the tracking of the surface \nfeatures/best forcing. If the frontal features track closer, this \ncould put southern New England in better forced environment and \npotentially with marginal instability present. This could favor a \nfew rumbles of thunders or heavier downpours. Timing of this is low \nconfidence due to the track uncertainties, but the general timeframe \nis late Thu through Sat. Lighter/spotty showers may linger at \nlate as Sunday. However, it likely won't be rain for the entire \ntimeframe. \n\nEnsemble guidance highlights probabilities of 30-60% for rain totals \nof 0.50\" or more and low probabilities (10-30%) for 1.0\" or greater. \nDeterministic solutions have leaned toward the upper percentile \nsolutions of their ensemble suite showing potential for amounts 1.0 \nto upward of 2.0\" inches. Once guidance comes into better agreement, \nconfidence will increase in the exact timing of rainfall and totals. \n\nAbove normal temperatures are likely Thursday with the mid-level \nthermal ridge shifting over southern New England with 925mb \ntemperatures +13-15C. This will support high temperatures in the \nupper 60s to low/mid 70s! Temperatures likely lean seasonably warm \nfor Friday as well. Depending on how the system tracks, we may get \none more day of milder temperatures Saturday with more\nseasonable temperatures arriving by Sunday.",
